  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nCool and dry northerly flow will continue for our region, due to\ncirculation around a stacked low pressure that is slow to \nprogress out of the Canadian Maritime provinces. With the chilly\nair aloft and small embedded shortwaves pinwheeling around the \nlow, minor instability during diurnal heating actually managed \nto generate isolated showers Saturday. Further drying of the \natmosphere continues to occur, so any isolated showers today \nwill probably be confined towards Catskills-Poconos if any at \nall. Sun will be mixed with a scattered cumulus field again as\ndiurnal heating occurs today.\n\nTemperatures in this Fall-ish pattern will be just a tad shy of\nclimatology today and Labor Day, with highs of upper 60s-mid \n70s and crisp mostly clear nights leading to lows of mid \n40s-lower 50s as high pressure drifts over the region. Currently\nwe still have some wind in the boundary layer and scattered \nclouds, so fog will tend to be very patchy. However, the next \ntwo nights will be clearer and with more decoupled to calm \nwinds, promoting better and more typical valley fog formation.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nSlight upper ridging begins Tuesday to start nudging\ntemperatures up, and then reaching us fully Wednesday with highs\nof mid 70s-lower 80s as warm air advection occurs ahead of an \napproaching front. The associated low pressure system forms in \nthe lee of the northern Rockies but moves north of the Great \nLakes Wednesday into Thursday. Thus the eventual cold front is\nprojected to trail pretty distantly from its parent low in\nCanada. Models have been trending a bit weaker with this front.\nThough the pattern suggests a fair amount of flow aloft and thus\nat least modest shear, ensemble data is now depicting very \nscant chances (less than 10 percent) of Convective Available\nPotential Energy of even reaching 400 J/Kg. There is a good\nchance of rain late Wednesday into Wednesday night, but it may\nbe via forced ascent from a strong jet to the north of the \nsurface front, and thus more of a stable ana-front rainfall. We\nwill continue to monitor but potential for stronger convection\nseems to be diminishing. There is uncertainty in the models for\nwhether a secondary front late Thursday-early Friday could\ngenerate a few showers, but high confidence that high pressure\nin its wake will probably allow dry cool weather towards the end\nof the week into early next weekend.",
